The molality of a 10%(v/v) solution of di-bromine solution in \(CCl_{4}\) (carbon tetrachloride) is 'x'.
x = \text{__________} \times 10^{-2} \text{M. (Nearest integer)}
[Given : molar mass of \(Br_{2}\) = 160 \(\text{g mol}^{-1}\), atomic mass of C = 12 \(\text{g mol}^{-1}\), atomic mass of Cl = 35.5 \(\text{g mol}^{-1}\), density of dibromine = 3.2 \(\text{g cm}^{-3}\), density of \(CCl_{4}\) = 1.6 \(\text{g cm}^{-3}\)]

questions.explanation:
A 10% (v/v) solution means 10 mL of solute (di-bromine) is present in 100 mL of solution. This implies the volume of the solvent (\(CCl_{4}\)) is 100 mL - 10 mL = 90 mL.
First, calculate the mass of the solute and the solvent using their given densities.
\text{Mass of solute (di-bromine)} = \text{Volume} \times \text{Density} = 10 \text{ mL} \times 3.2 \text{ g/mL} = 32 \text{ g}
\text{Mass of solvent (CCl}_4\text{)} = \text{Volume} \times \text{Density} = 90 \text{ mL} \times 1.6 \text{ g/mL} = 144 \text{ g}
Now, calculate the molality (m) using the formula:
m = \frac{\text{moles of solute}}{\text{mass of solvent (kg)}} = \frac{\text{mass of solute (g)} \times 1000}{\text{molar mass of solute} \times \text{mass of solvent (g)}}
m = \frac{32 \times 1000}{160 \times 144} = \frac{32000}{23040} \approx 1.388 \text{ m}
The problem states that the molality is given by \(x \times 10^{-2}\). We need to find the value of x.
1.388 = x \times 10^{-2}
x = 1.388 \times 10^{2} = 138.8
The question asks for the nearest integer value of x.
x \approx 139

